@subsection Expressions

One easy way to get started at programming is to examine some typical
interactions with OCaml.  Imagine that you are sitting at a computer
terminal.  You type an @newterm{expression}, and the toplevel responds by
displaying the result of its @newterm{evaluating} that expression.

One kind of primitive expression you might type is a number.  (More precisely,
the expression that you type consists of the numerals that represent the number
in base 10.)  If you present the toplevel with a number

@example ocaml
486;;
(* => 486 *)
@end example

@noindent
the toplevel will respond by printing it.@footnote{Throughout this book, when
we wish to emphasize the distinction between the input typed by the user and
the response printed by the system, we show the response as a comment line
beginning with @code{=>}.  A live toplevel also announces the type of every
result; the primer's transcripts show those replies, and we omit the type
here so that the listings stay close to the book's shape.}

Expressions representing numbers may be combined with an expression
representing a primitive operation (such as @code{+} or @code{*}) to form a
compound expression that represents the application of the operation to those
numbers.  For @w{example}:

@example ocaml
137 + 349;;
(* => 486 *)
@end example

@example ocaml
1000 - 334;;
(* => 666 *)
@end example

@example ocaml
5 * 99;;
(* => 495 *)
@end example

@example ocaml
10 / 5;;
(* => 2 *)
@end example

@example ocaml
2.7 +. 10.0;;
(* => 12.7 *)
@end example

@noindent
The last interaction opens a topic that will shape every numerical program
in this book.  OCaml distinguishes the integers of the footnote above from
real numbers, which it represents as @newterm{floating-point numbers}, and
it gives each world its own operators: @code{+}, @code{-}, @code{*}, and
@code{/} work only on integers, while @code{+.}, @code{-.}, @code{*.}, and
@code{/.} work only on floating-point numbers.  Writing @code{2.7 + 10} is
not a calculation but a @newterm{type error}: the compiler rejects the
program before anything runs.  Crossing between the two worlds is always an
explicit conversion, most often @code{Float.of_int}, and this book never
performs one silently.

Expressions such as these, formed by writing an operator and its operands
together, are called @newterm{combinations}.  In @code{137 + 349} the
element that specifies the operation is called the @newterm{operator}, and
the elements it is applied to are called @newterm{operands}.  The value of
a combination is obtained by applying the operation specified by the
operator to the @newterm{arguments} that are the values of the operands.

Where the book's Scheme places the operator to the left of its operands, a
convention known as @newterm{prefix notation}, OCaml writes a binary
operator between them, a convention known as @newterm{infix notation}.  The
change is mostly cosmetic, but it has two
consequences worth pausing over.  The first is that an infix operator
combines a fixed number of operands, usually two, so adding four numbers
takes three @code{+}s, chained left to right:

@example ocaml
21 + 35 + 12 + 7;;
(* => 75 *)
@end example

@example ocaml
25 * 4 * 12;;
(* => 1200 *)
@end example

@noindent
No ambiguity can arise: each operator combines exactly the two operands
beside it, and the chain groups the same way every time.

The second consequence is that operators carry a @newterm{precedence},
which says how expressions group when the reader writes no parentheses.
Multiplication binds tighter than addition, so some of the book's
parentheses become optional while others remain essential.  Nesting
combinations still works exactly as it did in the prefix notation:

@example ocaml
3 * 5 + (10 - 6);;
(* => 19 *)
@end example

@noindent
Here the parentheses are not load-bearing: addition and subtraction share
precedence and associate to the left, so @code{3 * 5 + 10 - 6} groups the
same way and comes to the same 19.  Parentheses become essential exactly
when they overrule that left-to-right reading, as when a subtraction is
itself subtracted:

@example ocaml
10 - (4 - 3);;
(* => 9 *)
@end example

@noindent
Dropping those parentheses, @code{10 - 4 - 3}, groups from the left instead
and comes to @code{3}; here the structure that the prefix form spelled out
explicitly is carried entirely by the parentheses, since precedence alone
does not settle it.

There is no limit (in principle) to the depth of such nesting and to the
overall complexity of the expressions that OCaml can evaluate.  It is we
humans who get confused by still relatively simple expressions such as

@example ocaml
3 * ((2 * 4) + (3 + 5)) + ((10 - 7) + 6);;
@end example

@noindent
which evaluates to be 57.  We can help ourselves by writing such an
expression in the form

@example ocaml
3 * ((2 * 4)
     + (3 + 5))
  + ((10 - 7)
     + 6);;
@end example

@noindent
following a formatting convention known as @newterm{pretty-printing}, in
which each long expression is written so that its subexpressions are
aligned vertically.  The resulting indentations display clearly the
structure of the expression.@footnote{OCaml systems typically provide
features to aid the user in formatting expressions.  Two especially useful
ones are an automatic indenter that keeps each subexpression aligned under
its operator, available in editors and in the @code{ocamlformat} tool that
formats this book's code, and a toplevel that points at the subexpression
involved in a type error.}

Even with complex expressions, the toplevel always operates in the same
basic cycle: It reads a phrase, evaluates the phrase, and prints the
result.  This mode of operation is often expressed by saying that the
toplevel runs in a @newterm{read-eval-print loop}.  Observe in particular
that it is not necessary to explicitly instruct the toplevel to print the
value of the expression.@footnote{OCaml inherits from Lisp the convention
that every expression has a value.  The double semicolon @code{;;} tells
the toplevel that the reader is done typing, and the reply names the
expression's type before its value.  That convention, together with the
old reputation of Lisp as an inefficient language, is the source of the
quip by Alan Perlis (paraphrasing Oscar Wilde) that ``Lisp programmers
know the value of everything but the cost of nothing.''  A typed language
replies to the quip by announcing the type of everything as well.}

